## Rules

1. **Tokens first** — use CSS variables (`rgb(var(--orbit-accent))`) not hardcoded hex in components.
2. **Theme-aware** — new UI must read palette variables so every color and game theme recolors it.
3. **Focusable everything** — interactive elements must register with `Focusable` or equivalent.
4. **Couch contrast** — keep text readable at a distance; test with `--orbit-muted` for secondary copy.
5. **Glass panels** — use `LiquidGlass` or `.macos-panel` / `.orbit-panel`; prefer `backdrop-filter` over opaque overlays.
6. **Document theme fields** — adding JSON theme properties requires updating [docs/THEMES.md](THEMES.md) and `ThemeDefinition` in `src/themes/types.ts` and `src-tauri/src/models/theme.rs` (plus sanitization in `services/theme_files.rs`).

## Settings viewport layout
Top-level navigation tabs have no Back button; detail flows retain Back. Settings
fills the space between shell header and footer with aligned panels. Only the middle
panel scrolls. Below 1180px the insights panel hides; below 520px tabs form a row
above the content. Windowed headers reserve titlebar space vertically, preserving logo alignment.

Reduce Motion replaces artwork immediately after it decodes. Reduce Transparency uses opaque glass panels.


### Portable glass materials

The shared `LiquidGlass` surface uses 12px toolbar blur and 8px panel blur,
subtle edge highlights, and the existing artwork-aware tint. Rust renders one
64×64 PNG grain tile on a blocking worker and caches it for the process. A single
bootstrap query shares the texture with the shell and portaled drawers.
All translucent UI surfaces share the same material. Panels and connected-library cards use the 8px panel treatment. Buttons, badges, and translucent text fields use a compact 6px control treatment. Opaque artwork faces and scene-dimming scrims keep their own paint.
There are no per-panel distortion maps, SVG refraction passes, or resize observers.
Reduced transparency, increased contrast, and forced colors switch to opaque
surfaces dynamically. Unsupported blur compositors use a near-opaque fill.

These are shared webview surfaces. Rust prepares their material texture. OS-native
window materials have platform-specific behavior and do not supply a common
per-panel API for the three supported desktop webviews.

The `orbit-glass-control` material class preserves semantic HTML controls and tinted selection states. The `orbit-panel` utility uses the same material as `LiquidGlass`. Dialogs, session panels, and quick menus share the texture and accessibility fallbacks.


### Image readiness and sizing

`ArtworkImage` reserves its container before loading, decodes asynchronously,
and crossfades only when ready. Failed images stop shimmering and expose an
accessible fallback. Theme cards align their 2:1 image regions independently of
title wrapping or selection state. Logos use containment; heroes use cover.
Native preview generation trims transparent logo padding before GPU scaling.

Bundled display assets and previews use WebP. Logos are alpha-trimmed and capped at 1024×512; wallpaper resolution is preserved up to 3840×2160. Custom image preparation runs on a Rust worker before download caching or saving.
